---
title: Chinese Eggplant with Garlic Sauce
description: Charred, soft Chinese eggplant with a thin cornstarch coat and a garlicky soy glaze.
tags: [stir-fry]
course: main
icons: [eggplant, garlic, bowl-chopsticks]
cuisine: Chinese
source:
  name: Omnivore's Cookbook
  url: https://omnivorescookbook.com/chinese-eggplant-with-garlic-sauce/
servings: 2
prep time: 25m
cook time: 15m
---

Option 1: Place the @Chinese eggplant{283%g}(10 oz, about 2 small, chopped to bite-size pieces) in a large #bowl{} and add water to cover. Add the @salt{1%tsp} and mix well. Place a #pot lid{} on top to keep the eggplant under water for ~soak{15%minutes}. Drain and pat dry.

Option 2: Spread the sliced eggplant out on a #paper towel{}. Sprinkle kosher salt on both sides of the eggplant slices. Allow to rest for ~rest{15%minutes}. Rinse with running tap water to wash off the salt, then pat dry thoroughly.

Combine the @light soy sauce{1%tbsp}(or soy sauce), @water{1%tbsp}, @dark soy sauce{1/2%tsp}, @sugar{2%tsp}, and @cornstarch{1%tsp} in a #small bowl{} and mix well.

Sprinkle the eggplant with @&cornstarch{1%tbsp} and mix by hand, until the eggplant is evenly coated with a thin layer of cornstarch.

Add 2 tablespoons of the @peanut oil{2%tbsp}(or vegetable oil) to a big nonstick #skillet{} and heat over medium-high heat until hot. Spread the eggplant across the bottom of the skillet without overlapping. Cook the eggplant one side at a time until all the surfaces are charred and the eggplant turns soft, ~cook{8-10%minutes} in total. Transfer the eggplant to a plate. If the skillet gets too hot and starts to smoke, turn to medium heat.

Add the remaining @&peanut oil{1/2%tbsp}, the @fresh ginger{1%tsp}(minced) and the @garlic cloves{3}(chopped) into the same skillet. Stir a few times until fragrant.

Add all the eggplant back into the skillet. Mix the sauce again until the cornstarch is fully dissolved and pour it over the eggplant. Immediately stir a few times, until the eggplant is evenly coated and the sauce thickens. Transfer everything to a big plate.

> **Serving** — Serve hot as a side or as a main over steamed rice or noodles.
